With that blocker resolved, running the test for the first time surfaced
two real logic bugs in the load test itself (not infra):

1. Broker-blip scenario never fired (purekafka.stream.restarts stayed 0).
   Added debug instrumentation (temporary, removed before this commit) and
   found the blip message (index 300) was being produced but never
   consumed within the test's time budget - consumption throughput
   (~17msg/s, due to per-message offset.commit + tracing-span overhead)
   is genuinely slower than production (~25msg/s), and a separate bug
   (below) meant the whole test was being killed by munit's actual 30s
   default IO-timeout before my own internal 32s budget ever got a
   chance to finish. Fixed by moving the blip trigger from message index
   400 to 300 (comfortably within reach) and, more importantly, fixing
   the timeout override itself: `munitTimeout` (what I'd originally
   overridden) doesn't bound a CatsEffectSuite's IO-based test body at
   all - decompiled CatsEffectSuite directly to confirm the actual method
   is `munitIOTimeout`, switched to overriding that instead.

2. Lag-gauge scenario never recorded a positive value
   (purekafka.consumer.lag stayed 0.0) even once the above was fixed.
   Root cause: Kafka's own `records-lag` metric only updates on an actual
   FetchResponse for that partition. My original "pause" mechanism
   blocked downstream processing (a Ref-gated wait inside the per-record
   handler), which backpressures fs2-kafka's own fetch loop too - no new
   fetches happen while paused, so the metric just freezes at whatever it
   last was instead of growing to reflect the real, growing gap. Fixed by
   replacing the pause with a burst: at t=15s (deliberately *after* the
   blip at message-index 300 has already been produced and consumed -
   an earlier attempt at t=5s pushed the blip's actual broker offset past
   what the consumer could reach in time, since the burst's 500 extra
   messages land on the same topic/offsets), fire 500 extra messages as
   fast as the producer can send them. This keeps the consumer's fetch
   loop actively polling throughout (no backpressure-freeze), so the next
   real FetchResponse legitimately reports the widened gap, which
   `ConsumerLag.fromConsumer` then correctly surfaces as a rising gauge
   value (confirmed via raw MetricData inspection during debugging,
   observed values up to ~50 before decaying back toward 0 as the
   consumer caught up).

